  "body": {
    "id": "prop:unbalanced-orbit-rank-degeneracy",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "Under the hypotheses and notation of Theorem \\(\\mathrm{thm{:}unbalanced\\text{-}frontier}\\), for every \\(G\\leq G_\\star^{(n_1,n_0)}\\), every \\(T:\\mathcal X_{n_1,n_0,K}\\to\\mathbb R\\), and every \\(C\\in\\mathcal X_{n_1,n_0,K}\\), the conventional nonrandomized orbit-rank value\n\\[\n |G|^{-1}\\bigl|\\{f\\in G:T(f(C))\\geq T(C)\\}\\bigr|\n\\]\nis identically \\(1\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "n1",
      "n0",
      "K",
      "G",
      "Gstar",
      "Tstat",
      "X",
      "C"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "thm:unbalanced-frontier"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Theorem \\(\\mathrm{thm{:}unbalanced\\text{-}frontier}\\) proves \\(G_\\star^{(n_1,n_0)}=\\{\\operatorname{id}\\}\\). Therefore every subgroup \\(G\\leq G_\\star^{(n_1,n_0)}\\) is \\(\\{\\operatorname{id}\\}\\), so \\(|G|=1\\), its orbit of every \\(C\\) is the singleton \\(\\{C\\}\\), and \\(T(\\operatorname{id}(C))=T(C)\\). Substitution into the displayed rank formula gives \\(1^{-1}|\\{\\operatorname{id}\\}|=1\\).",
    "justification": "This records the requested unequal-arm consequence of the identity maximal common group.",
    "gap": "Without this corollary, the orbit-rank narrative would stop at the balanced \\(\\{1/2,1\\}\\) granularity despite the discharged unequal-arm classification.",
    "consumer": "It tells design-audit software that changing only the arm allocation from equal to unequal removes even the nontrivial common orbit.",
